From 81667e532b1f5ba46dd7ee916e486eaf8d948e62 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JustFoura <JustFoura.dev@proton.me>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 16:03:02 +1300
Subject: [PATCH 17/18] Prefill copy stream: retire before flushing, drain the
 batch concurrently

Picks up the win the copy-pool commit had already written off. Prefill goes
172 -> 202 t/s (+17%) with parity byte-identical on every arm.

1. reserve() flushed before it retired. At the moment the pool ran dry,
   copy_batch held exactly one un-submitted slot, so every reclaim pushed a
   one-member batch: avg_batch was pinned at 1 by the recovery path itself.
   Retire the oldest in-flight batch first and only submit the open batch when
   nothing is left in flight to reclaim. avg_batch 1.1 -> 7.7, blocked 12,632
   -> 1,777.

   This falsifies "copies and compute contexts alternate ~1:1, there is no bulk
   copy phase". The flush blamed for it (get_compute_ctx, ~7,420 calls/pass,
   one per graph node) is a no-op -- the scheduler issues every copy between
   graph computes, so copy_batch is empty during node iteration. flush_by
   counters now prove the distribution: get_compute_ctx=0, batch_full=1650.

2. Drain a whole batch concurrently. in_memcpys were drained one memcpy at a
   time, a queue-depth-1 fault stream, while a batch already holds up to
   copy_batch_max independent copies each writing its own staging slot.
   LLAMA_VK_COPY_DRAIN_THREADS (default 8; 1 = old sequential drain).
   170.5/141.3 t/s -> 188.7/183.0 t/s order-controlled. 16+ threads regress
   on 8 physical cores.

3. Drop the 4 MiB pread floor to 256 KiB. The floor excluded most of the
   stream -- the copy histogram averages 3.4 MB -- and was set when each copy
   paid for its own thread setup, which a batched drain now amortises.
   LLAMA_VK_COPY_PREAD_MIN_KB. 177.5/193.7 -> 202.2/201.3 t/s, drain rate
   2.5-2.7 -> 3.2 GB/s.

Also adds flush_by attribution counters, drain/retire/bytes accounting, and
fixes the harness's gpu_busy seconds print (ms/s mixed).

The pass is now 74 s = 48.8 s GPU busy + 14.8 s drain + ~10 s other, and
retire_fence is 0.0 s on every arm -- the copy path no longer blocks on the
GPU. 14,960 tok / 48.8 s = 306 t/s is the GPU floor, so the remaining gap to
the 300 target is entirely the drain still sitting on the critical path.

